            <ArticleTitle>Fourteen-year experience with laparoscopic ventrosuspension in patients with retroverted and retroflected uterus and pelvic pain syndromes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>749-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jmig.2010.07.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In women with a retroverted uterus, who have dyspareunia, chronic pelvic pain, or dysmenorrhea, laparoscopic ventrosuspension of the uterus has been reported effective in achieving symptom relief.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To critically review our experience with our method of laparoscopic ventrosuspension.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cohort study (Canadian Task Force classification II-3).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary care center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty-three women who had undergone laparoscopic ventrosuspension for treatment of pain syndromes during 1995 through 2008.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic ventrosuspension, and a questionnaire about the long-term outcome of the operation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were no adverse events except for 2 repeat operations within 3 postoperative days. Forty-nine women (77.8%) answered the questionnaire about long-term outcome, and in these patients, significant pain relief was achieved (p &lt;.001). Pain levels decreased, based on a numeric rating scale, from a mean (SD) of 6.35 (1.92) to 0.97 (1.40) in patients without endometriosis, and from 6.93 (2.09) to 3.80 (2.08) in those with endometriosis. Of 34 patients without endometriosis, 1 (2.9%) stated that the operation had not led to symptom relief, compared with 4 of 15 (26.7%) with endometriosis (p = .03).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Laparoscopic ventrosuspension is clearly beneficial in women with a retroverted and retroflected uterus who have pelvic pain syndromes, even in the long term.</AbstractText>
